    mistakes:["Capitalising the species name (it's always lowercase).","Writing the genus without a capital.","Thinking organisms that look alike are always closely related."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Botany and zoology, SANBI biodiversity research, conservation, agriculture (identifying pests) and medicine (identifying bacteria)."},
    activity:"Make a simple dichotomous key for five objects in your pencil case.",
    activityAnswer:"Example: 1a writes → 2; 1b doesn't write → 3. 2a uses ink → pen; 2b graphite → pencil. 3a cuts → scissors; 3b measures → ruler; 3c removes marks → eraser.",
    examTip:"When writing a scientific name by hand, underline genus and species separately (e.g. <u>Homo</u> <u>sapiens</u>).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Binomial nomenclature",d:"Two-part scientific naming system (Genus species)"},{w:"Species",d:"Organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring"},{w:"Dichotomous key",d:"Identification tool using pairs of choices"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Which is written correctly?",opts:["Homo Sapiens","homo sapiens","Homo sapiens","HOMO SAPIENS"],a:2,et:"Genus capitalised, species lowercase, italicised."},
      {q:"The smallest group in the hierarchy:",opts:["Genus","Family","Species","Order"],a:2,et:"Species is the most specific level."},
      {q:"Two organisms in the same genus are:",opts:["Unrelated","Closely related","The same species","In different kingdoms"],a:1,et:"Sharing a genus means a recent common ancestor."},
    ],
